insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('10ad0464-bddc-55d1-8c68-7e9652000459', 'wg10-paragraph-transitions-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g10-paragraph-transitions'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Learning Stop-Motion the Hard Way$q$, $q$(1) Stop-motion animation creates the illusion of movement from thousands of still photographs. (2) An animator positions a clay figure or puppet, takes a picture, moves the figure a tiny amount, and takes another picture. (3) When the photographs are played back quickly, usually at twelve to twenty-four frames per second, the viewer's eye blends them into smooth motion. (4) ____, a single second of film can require two dozen separate poses, each one adjusted by hand.

(5) Our school's animation club learned this lesson during our first project, a ninety-second film about a paper boat sailing through a storm. (6) We began by sketching a storyboard, a series of rough drawings showing each shot in order. (7) Next, we built the boat, the waves, and a small cardboard harbor. (8) ____, we spent almost three weeks of afternoons filming. (9) ____, the most frustrating problem turned out to be the lighting rather than the puppets. (10) Each time a cloud passed outside the window, the brightness of the room changed, and the finished frames flickered like a broken television.

(11) For our second film, we covered the windows with black paper and used only lamps we could control. (12) We also taped the camera tripod to the floor so that no one could bump it between shots. (13) Modeling clay is made from fine mineral particles mixed with water or oil. (14) These small changes made the new film look far more polished than the first, even though we had not become much faster at moving the puppets.$q$, 'draft')
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body
